# What each filter is, as the family answers it.
#
# A filter is a member of a family of seventeen, and the family is asked
# the same questions of every member: can it be constructed from a name
# alone, or does it need a parameter dictionary first, and is it in
# every build or only one made with a particular library. Both are
# differences a program sees -- a name that answers undefined here is a
# name a program cannot use -- and neither was written down anywhere
# before this file.
#
# The reason to hold a whole family rather than each member where it is
# used: the last defect here was a lazy end-of-data desynchronisation,
# and it was in ALL FIVE stream decoders at once, because each was
# written by copying the one before. A question asked of one member and
# not the rest is how that happens.
#
# Membership is DERIVED, from the names the filter operator compares
# against in src/lib/xpost_op_file.c, and held to this file in both
# directions. A filter added to the interpreter and not classified here
# fails, and a line here naming a filter the interpreter does not know
# fails; the second is what stops a line outliving what it described.
#
# Each line is
#
#   <name>  <plain|needsdict>  <always|HAVE_SYMBOL>  <reason>
#
# plain      constructible as `file /Name filter`
# needsdict  refuses that and wants `file dict /Name filter`; the reason
#            says which parameters it cannot be built without
# always     present in every build
# HAVE_x     present only where the build found that library, and the
#            source guards it with exactly that symbol, which is checked
#
# The reason is required on every line, including the plain ones, and
# says what the filter is for. A line nobody could explain is a member
# nobody examined.

ASCIIHexEncode        plain      always        writes each byte as two hexadecimal digits, PLRM 3.13.3
ASCIIHexDecode        plain      always        reads hexadecimal digit pairs back to bytes, PLRM 3.13.3
ASCII85Encode         plain      always        writes four bytes as five printable characters, PLRM 3.13.3
ASCII85Decode         plain      always        reads that encoding back, PLRM 3.13.3
NullEncode            plain      always        passes bytes through unchanged; the family's identity, and PLRM gives it no decoding counterpart
RunLengthEncode       plain      always        writes runs as a count and a byte, PLRM 3.13.3
RunLengthDecode       plain      always        reads those runs back, PLRM 3.13.3
LZWEncode             plain      always        writes the LZW encoding, PLRM 3.13.3
LZWDecode             plain      always        reads it back, PLRM 3.13.3
CCITTFaxEncode        plain      always        writes the CCITT Group 3 and 4 facsimile encodings, PLRM 3.13.3
CCITTFaxDecode        plain      always        reads them back, PLRM 3.13.3
FlateEncode           plain      HAVE_ZLIB     writes the deflate encoding, PLRM 3.13.3, and the deflater is the library's
FlateDecode           plain      HAVE_ZLIB     reads it back, from the same library
DCTEncode             needsdict  HAVE_LIBJPEG  cannot be built from a name: the encoder is told the image geometry it is compressing, and Columns, Rows and Colors have no default a filter could pick
DCTDecode             plain      HAVE_LIBJPEG  needs no such dictionary, because the geometry it decompresses is carried in the stream it reads
SubFileDecode         needsdict  always        cannot be built from a name: what ends the subfile is EODCount and EODString, and a filter with neither has no end
ReusableStreamDecode  plain      always        holds what it read so the stream can be read again, PLRM 3.13.2

